{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 9, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "lambda(8) = 2 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 8 = 2^3; lambda is the lcm of 2 (2^3)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(8) = 4\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 2\n"}], "structuredContent": {"id": 9, "ok": true, "cmd": "carmichael", "ucas": "carmichael 8", "structured": true, "result": {"kind": "carmichael", "method": "8 = 2^3; lambda is the lcm of 2 (2^3)", "lambda(2^e)": "2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(8) = 4", "headline": "lambda(8) = 2 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)"}, "notes": [], "conditions": [], "checks": [{"kind": "check", "passed": true, "text": "the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 2"}], "files": [], "text": "lambda(8) = 2 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 8 = 2^3; lambda is the lcm of 2 (2^3)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(8) = 4\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 2\n"}, "isError": false}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 10, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80\n"}], "structuredContent": {"id": 10, "ok": true, "cmd": "carmichael", "ucas": "carmichael 561", "structured": true, "result": {"kind": "carmichael", "method": "561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)", "lambda(2^e)": "2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320", "headline": "lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)"}, "notes": [], "conditions": [], "checks": [{"kind": "check", "passed": true, "text": "the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80"}], "files": [], "text": "lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80\n"}, "isError": false}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 11, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "lambda(240) = 4 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 240 = 2^4 * 3 * 5; lambda is the lcm of 4 (2^4), 2 (3^1), 4 (5^1)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(240) = 64\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 4\n"}], "structuredContent": {"id": 11, "ok": true, "cmd": "carmichael", "ucas": "carmichael 240", "structured": true, "result": {"kind": "carmichael", "method": "240 = 2^4 * 3 * 5; lambda is the lcm of 4 (2^4), 2 (3^1), 4 (5^1)", "lambda(2^e)": "2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(240) = 64", "headline": "lambda(240) = 4 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)"}, "notes": [], "conditions": [], "checks": [{"kind": "check", "passed": true, "text": "the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 4"}], "files": [], "text": "lambda(240) = 4 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)\n  how: 240 = 2^4 * 3 * 5; lambda is the lcm of 4 (2^4), 2 (3^1), 4 (5^1)\n  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(240) = 64\n  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 4\n"}, "isError": false}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 12, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "1 = 1\n  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)\n  check: the product is 1; each factor is prime\n"}], "structuredContent": {"id": 12, "ok": true, "cmd": "factorint", "ucas": "factorint 1", "structured": true, "result": {"kind": "factorint", "method": "trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)", "lhs": "1", "value": "1", "value_int": 1, "headline": "1 = 1"}, "notes": [], "conditions": [], "checks": [{"kind": "check", "passed": true, "text": "the product is 1; each factor is prime"}], "files": [], "text": "1 = 1\n  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)\n  check: the product is 1; each factor is prime\n"}, "isError": false}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 13, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "561 = 3 * 11 * 17 (not prime)\n  a Carmichael number: a^(n-1) = 1 for every a coprime to it (lambda(561) = 80 divides n - 1), so Fermat's test is fooled\n  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)\n  check: the product is 561; each factor is prime\n"}], "structuredContent": {"id": 13, "ok": true, "cmd": "factorint", "ucas": "factorint 561", "structured": true, "result": {"kind": "factorint", "a_carmichael_number": "a^(n-1) = 1 for every a coprime to it (lambda(561) = 80 divides n - 1), so Fermat's test is fooled", "method": "trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)", "lhs": "561", "annotation": "not prime", "value": "3 * 11 * 17", "headline": "561 = 3 * 11 * 17 (not prime)"}, "notes": [], "conditions": [], "checks": [{"kind": "check", "passed": true, "text": "the product is 561; each factor is prime"}], "files": [], "text": "561 = 3 * 11 * 17 (not prime)\n  a Carmichael number: a^(n-1) = 1 for every a coprime to it (lambda(561) = 80 divides n - 1), so Fermat's test is fooled\n  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)\n  check: the product is 561; each factor is prime\n"}, "isError": false}}
